  "query_preview": "..\n\nUpper ridge over the northern plains will slowly drift eastward\nthrough the weekend as an upper trough digs into the western\nstates. Southwest flow and weak embedded shortwaves will bring\nperiodic chances for rain into the region. \n\nLight rain will continue to slowly move northeast through the \nevening and linger overnight. Most locations look to have a damp \nand chilly Halloween evening. Fog will continue to impact portions\nof the region through the morning with some locally dense fog, \nreducing visibility in spots to 2 mile or less.\n\nAs the Pacific upper trough moves through the Great Basin, a\nColorado low is expected to develop ahead of it. This will spread\nprecipitation northward, and will generally favor the Yellowstone\nbasin for Sunday night into Monday. Splitting in the upper trough\nnear the Canadian boarder will keep cold air from being drawn\nsouthward, and result in little to no mixed precipitation or snow.\nAs the surface low deepens, an increase in winds will result by \nMonday afternoon.\n\nA progressive, unsettled pattern will keep next week interesting\nwith repeated upper level troughs.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nAdjusted PoPs by blending SREF for slightly expanded coverage. \nOtherwise no changes to NBM.\n\nA lot of uncertainty regarding fog, low cloud and precipitation\ntoday. \n\nSystem for Monday continues to slide southward, which is common,\nand could result in less precipitation, especially across the\nnorth, than expected.",
